1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:04,850
Closure's public pitch is that the AI finds the material, and people check it.

2
00:00:04,975 --> 00:00:09,562
That evidence still has to survive court, and one invented quote could cost trust

3
00:00:09,562 --> 00:00:11,125
with many agencies.

4
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:13,900
Cite or Refuse is built on one rule.

5
00:00:14,037 --> 00:00:16,925
Every claim links to an exact line in the record.

6
00:00:17,025 --> 00:00:20,425
If it can't be traced, it's refused, and you see why.

7
00:00:20,613 --> 00:00:22,925
All of it runs on synthetic data.

8
00:00:24,075 --> 00:00:25,425
Here's the big picture.

9
00:00:25,538 --> 00:00:28,150
A seeded generator writes a fictional case.

10
00:00:28,275 --> 00:00:31,825
The chunker splits it into windows that keep their source metadata.

11
00:00:31,962 --> 00:00:36,400
A BM25 retriever finds the best chunks, the answerer drafts quoted

12
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,325
claims, and the verifier checks each one.

13
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:42,675
FastAPI serves it to one static page.

14
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:46,825
And the eval calls the same pipeline, so you measure what users see.

15
00:00:47,925 --> 00:00:52,475
The case is forty jail calls and fifteen case files, from a fixed seed.

16
00:00:52,625 --> 00:00:56,400
The chunker slides a four-line window, three lines at a time.

17
00:00:56,538 --> 00:01:01,275
Every chunk keeps what's needed to cite it: a call ID with start and end seconds,

18
00:01:01,388 --> 00:01:03,475
or a page and line numbers.

19
00:01:03,587 --> 00:01:06,500
The citation label is computed from those fields.

20
00:01:06,613 --> 00:01:07,800
Nobody types it.

21
00:01:08,925 --> 00:01:10,350
Let's trace one question.

22
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:12,925
What did Andre tell Rico to do with the green car?

23
00:01:13,050 --> 00:01:15,150
The page posts it to /ask.

24
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:17,700
The retriever keeps the top five chunks.

25
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:21,950
The mock answerer scores each line by how much of the question's rare vocabulary it

26
00:01:21,950 --> 00:01:22,775
covers.

27
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:27,825
Three lines clear the bar: the same instruction from two near-duplicate calls, plus

28
00:01:27,825 --> 00:01:29,775
the car's registration record.

29
00:01:29,938 --> 00:01:34,925
All three pass the verifier, and the request is logged to runs.jsonl.

30
00:01:36,025 --> 00:01:39,325
The verifier is plain code, and the first failure wins.

31
00:01:39,462 --> 00:01:42,025
One: does the citation point at real lines?

32
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:43,925
If not, it's a bad citation.

33
00:01:44,075 --> 00:01:45,650
Two: is the quote verbatim?

34
00:01:45,775 --> 00:01:48,425
Only whitespace and curly quotes are normalized.

35
00:01:48,550 --> 00:01:50,100
Three: is it supported?

36
00:01:50,225 --> 00:01:54,175
The speaker must match, every number and name must be in the span, and word overlap

37
00:01:54,175 --> 00:01:56,125
must reach 0.6.

38
00:01:56,262 --> 00:02:00,712
In the demo, inject a fault, and call seventeen's timestamp moves past the end of

39
00:02:00,712 --> 00:02:01,375
the call.

40
00:02:01,488 --> 00:02:03,125
Refused, with the reason.

41
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:08,350
The eval runs the same pipeline over thirty gold questions, five of them

42
00:02:08,350 --> 00:02:09,475
unanswerable.

43
00:02:09,613 --> 00:02:14,025
In mock mode, recall@5 is one, every shown quote is verbatim, and every

44
00:02:14,025 --> 00:02:16,225
unanswerable question is refused.

45
00:02:16,362 --> 00:02:21,050
Then fault injection corrupts a fifth of the claims: a wrong timestamp, an altered

46
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:22,925
quote, or a swapped speaker.

47
00:02:23,062 --> 00:02:25,175
The verifier catches nine of nine.

48
00:02:25,325 --> 00:02:30,050
Citation precision is only 0.68, because the mock sometimes quotes a

49
00:02:30,050 --> 00:02:31,175
related line.

50
00:02:32,325 --> 00:02:33,350
Some gotchas.

51
00:02:33,463 --> 00:02:38,100
In mock mode the claim is built from the quote, so a clean claim always scores one.

52
00:02:38,225 --> 00:02:42,700
The support check really matters against faults, or a model that paraphrases.

53
00:02:42,837 --> 00:02:47,650
Verbatim matching assumes clean transcripts; speech-to-text errors would break it.

54
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:52,350
And the verifier proves traceable, not true: the green car and the blue pickup

55
00:02:52,350 --> 00:02:53,825
witnesses both pass.

56
00:02:54,425 --> 00:02:59,700
Next steps: dense retrieval beside BM25, comparing models on the same

57
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:04,175
eval, fuzzy matching, and a bigger gold set, written by someone else.

58
00:03:05,325 --> 00:03:09,100
To run it, install the requirements and run python run.py.

59
00:03:09,262 --> 00:03:11,900
Then open localhost, port eight thousand.

60
00:03:12,038 --> 00:03:13,025
No key needed.

61
00:03:13,125 --> 00:03:16,225
You get three tabs: Ask, Source viewer, and Eval.

62
00:03:16,325 --> 00:03:19,275
To try a real model, set one environment variable.

63
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:23,800
The model only picks lines by ID; our metadata builds the citation.

64
00:03:23,938 --> 00:03:29,050
To recap: citations come from metadata, string matching decides if a quote exists,

65
00:03:29,125 --> 00:03:32,225
refusals stay visible, and the eval measures it all.
